1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,177
Day 57: QueryLM, a 360 million parameter model pretrained from

2
00:00:05,177 --> 00:00:08,517
scratch to write its own search queries,

3
00:00:08,517 --> 00:00:10,437
retested on a local PC.

4
00:00:10,887 --> 00:00:14,557
QueryLM was pretrained on Wikipedia text where some positions

5
00:00:14,557 --> 00:00:17,986
were replaced with a search query followed by a retrieved

6
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:18,467
passage.

7
00:00:18,467 --> 00:00:20,994
The model only learned to write the query,

8
00:00:20,994 --> 00:00:23,159
never to predict the passage itself.

9
00:00:23,609 --> 00:00:27,766
Given a question, the model can open a search block and

10
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:30,714
write its own query, at most 24 tokens.

11
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:33,068
The caller runs any retriever and

12
00:00:33,068 --> 00:00:35,319
inserts the passage back into the text.

13
00:00:35,319 --> 00:00:38,781
The model was never trained to generate that passage itself.

14
00:00:39,231 --> 00:00:42,810
With the passage in context, the model keeps generating and

15
00:00:42,810 --> 00:00:44,084
answers the question.

16
00:00:44,534 --> 00:00:47,850
On 8 questions, searching seemed to hurt:

17
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:49,711
3 correct dropped to 2.

18
00:00:49,711 --> 00:00:54,645
Rerunning on CPU and GPU in two precisions gave 3 every time.

19
00:00:54,645 --> 00:00:59,580
On 300 questions, search lifted QueryLM from 1 to 17 correct.

20
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:02,928
The chat fine-tuned version went from 3 to 80.

21
00:01:02,928 --> 00:01:06,015
Full results and sources are in the Zenn article,

22
00:01:06,015 --> 00:01:06,834
linked below.
